{-
   The unsafe entrance to `Atomic`, and why it is here rather than there.

   Dedicated `Atomic(a)` storage is right for safe code and insufficient for everything whose address
   and layout are imposed from outside: a memory-mapped file, a shared-memory ABI, a device ring, a
   kernel structure. This is the one operation that turns bytes at an address into an atomic, and it
   is in **Native** because that is where the language keeps what it cannot check - a program reaching
   for this writes `import Native` and says so at every call site.

   The file is `.native.yana` because `Atomic(a)`'s operations are, and there is no point admitting a
   view on a target that has nothing to do with it.
-}
import Atomic

{-
  Existing storage, and the one unchecked way in - Analysis-Atomics.md §3.7.

  A **view** of the bytes at an address - not a new atomic, not an allocation, and not a copy.
  Everything in `Atomic` then applies to the borrow it answers, so there is one semantic surface and
  one lowering rather than a second family of pointer intrinsics.

  What the caller promises, none of which anything checks:

    1. the address is valid for the whole of the view's lifetime;
    2. it carries `Atomic(a)`'s alignment, which is the access width;
    3. the bytes there are an initialized `a`; and
    4. every concurrent access to those bytes uses an atomic view of the same width.

  The fourth is the one that is easy to lose. Two views of different widths over overlapping bytes
  are not atomic with respect to each other on any target, and neither is an atomic view beside a
  plain read of the same location - which is a data race in the model's own terms, whatever the
  hardware happens to do with it.

  `return address` is what roots the result: the borrow's extent is the pointer's, so the ordinary
  borrow rules apply to it from here on and nothing about it is special except how it was obtained.
  That is `borrow` above with the type fixed, and deliberately so.

  **Atomic access is not volatile access.** The optimizer may remove an atomic operation wherever the
  memory model proves it unobservable; a volatile device access says the access *itself* is the
  observable event. A location needing both wants a future operation that says both, not this one and
  a hope that the first implies the second.

  **And atomic visibility is not durability.** A release store has ordered CPU memory; it has not
  flushed a cache line, issued a device barrier, or made anything survive power loss.
-}
{-
   `%source'U8 -> source'Atomic(a)` - the pointee names the origin and the result is in it, which is
   what every other pointer-rooted function in the library writes.

   **This was the last `return` marker in `lib/`, and the argument for keeping it did not survive
   being measured.** The note that stood here said a *labelled* group stretches each member's loan to
   the last use of every other member, so two views of one atomic through one pointer would keep each
   other's shared loans alive and a `store` through the first would be a write to storage the second
   borrows - which is exactly what an atomic is for, and exactly what the anonymous group permits. It
   named `test/resolve/Atomic.yana`'s `viewed` as the observation and said it would fail the moment
   this line changed.

   It does not. `viewed` compiles, so does a stronger form of it whose pointer actually carries an
   origin for `source` to bind to (`viewed`'s is `null()`, so nothing was ever bound), and so does
   every fixture in `test/resolve`, `test/lib` and `test/bench` - swept under both spellings, zero
   differences. Nor does the mechanism behave as described: two members of one labelled group with a
   write between them, where the first member's last use is *before* the write, is accepted, while
   moving the write to where the borrow is genuinely live is refused. An extent is a live range per
   loan, not per group.

   So the anonymous group was never carrying the weight this attributed to it. What may be carrying
   it instead is that an atomic `store` does not reach the loan checker as a write *through the
   view* at all, in which case the spelling is free because the question is not being asked rather
   than because the answer is yes. That is worth knowing if a future model does ask it - the gap the
   old note pointed at, a model that can say "shared, and writable anyway", is real whatever this
   line says.
-}
@builtin(atomicAt) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) atomicAt(address: %source'U8) -> source'Atomic(a)
